EXT. HOMESTEAD - DAWN

A sod house on an empty plain. Wind combs the grass. A broken wagon wheel leans against the door. A lone windmill creaks.

NELL TREMAINE-ADEYEMI, 40s, stands in the yard in a man's coat and a faded bonnet. A rifle rests against the fence post beside her. A saddlebag, half packed, lies in the dirt at her feet. She does not look at the road.

She crouches. Pulls a letter from her pocket. Smooths it against her knee.

NELL (V.O.)
(reading, as if written)
"Dear Nell. By the time you read this, I'll be in Cheyenne."

She lowers the letter. Looks at the horizon. Raises it again.

NELL (V.O.) (CONT'D)
"The claim is yours. The mule, too. I never did like him."

A small, dry breath, almost a laugh. She tucks the letter into her coat.

She stands. Walks to the well. Lowers the bucket on its rope. The rope creaks. She draws it up slowly, hand over hand, never taking her eyes off the plain.

Drinks from her cupped palm. Wipes her chin.

INT. HOMESTEAD - CONTINUOUS

A single room. Table, stove, cot. A tintype of two people on the wall: Nell, younger, and a man with a stern moustache. She takes it down. Looks at it. Wipes it with her sleeve. Places it face-down on the table.

NELL
(to the empty room)
Well.

She opens a tin on the shelf. Counts coins. Not many. She puts them in her pocket.

On the stove, a pot of coffee. She pours a cup, holds it, doesn't drink. Sets it on the sill by the window. Steam rises into the dawn.

EXT. HOMESTEAD - CONTINUOUS

She walks to the barn. Opens the door. A mule stands in the dark, chewing hay.

NELL
Morning, you.

The mule flicks an ear.

NELL (CONT'D)
You heard?

The mule turns away. She laughs, once, helplessly.

NELL (CONT'D)
He said you were his favourite.

She leads the mule out. Loads the saddlebag. Tightens the cinch with hard jerks.

NELL (V.O.)
(reading)
"Don't wait for me. Don't write. If you must hate someone, hate the weather."

She stops. Hands flat on the mule's flank.

NELL (V.O.) (CONT'D)
"I was never any good at stopping."

She turns. The sun clears the horizon and the whole plain turns gold. She stands in the yard with the mule and the rifle and the house behind her.

She picks up the rifle. Aims at nothing. At the windmill. At the sky.

Lowers it.

NELL (CONT'D)
(quietly)
Fine.

She walks to the house. Takes the tintype from the table, wraps it in a rag, tucks it in the saddlebag. Pulls the saddlebag tight.

INT. HOMESTEAD - CONTINUOUS

The coffee cup on the sill has gone cold. She takes it. Pours it out on the floor. Turns the cup upside down on the table.

EXT. HOMESTEAD - CONTINUOUS

She mounts the mule. Looks at the house one last time. The windmill creaks. The wheel leans against the door.

She turns the mule toward the road. Not Cheyenne. The other way.

NELL
Come on.

They go. The grass closes behind them.

NELL (V.O.)
(reading, fading)
"Yours, always. Thomas."

The windmill turns. The yard is empty.
